{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The {{Nihongo|'''House of Commoners'''|衆議院|Shūgiin}} is the [[lower house]] of the [[National Diet]] of [[Okaiken]]. The [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] is the [[upper house]].

The House of Commoners has 475 members, elected for a four-year term. Of these, 289 are elected from single-member constituencies under first-past-the-post, and 186 are elected from 11 regional proportional representation blocks. The most recent general election was held on [[Okaiken general election, 2026|17 May 2026]], which produced a left-wing coalition government led by Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] (SCP). The house is the more powerful of the two houses, able to override vetoes on bills imposed by the House of Officers with a two-thirds majority. It can be dissolved by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] at will. The most recent dissolution was by Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] on 12 March 2026, following the passage of a motion of no confidence during the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident|"March Maelstrom."]]

== Powers ==
The House of Commoners is the more powerful chamber of the Diet, possessing powers that are not granted to the House of Officers.

* '''Legislation:''' If a bill is passed by the House of Commoners but is voted down, amended, or not acted upon within 60 days by the House of Officers, the bill is returned to the House of Commoners. The Commoners can then pass the bill into law by securing a two-thirds majority of those present.
* '''Budget and Treaties:''' The budget and the ratification of international treaties must be introduced in the House of Commoners first. If the House of Officers disagrees with the House of Commoners, the decision of the Commoners becomes the decision of the Diet after 30 days, or immediately if a joint committee fails to reach an agreement.
* '''Designation of the Prime Minister:''' The House of Commoners has the final say in appointing the Prime Minister. If the two houses cannot agree on a candidate, the choice of the House of Commoners prevails.
* '''Confidence and No-Confidence:''' The House of Commoners is the only chamber that can hold the government to account via a motion of no-confidence. If a no-confidence motion is passed, the Cabinet must resign ''en masse'', or the Prime Minister must dissolve the House of Commoners within ten days, triggering a general election.

== History ==
=== Post-war LDP dominance ===
For most of the late 20th century, Okaiken politics was dominated by the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party (LDP)]], which held a near-unbroken streak in government from 1972. This period was marked by rapid economic growth but also by political stagnation.

=== 2016 Election: The Red Reckoning ===
The LDP's dominance was shattered in the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]]. Wracked by corruption scandals and a damaging internal leadership crisis, the LDP collapsed to third place. The [[Sanese Communist Party|Sanese Communist Party (SCP)]], led by [[Sho Aoyagi]], won a historic plurality, forming a left-wing coalition with the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party of Okaiken (PPO)]] and [[Honda Shigetsuma]].

=== 2021 Election: The Progressive Pivot ===
The Aoyagi government (2016–2021) was turbulent, plagued by high inflation and coalition infighting. In the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]], the PPO, led by Honda, surged to 201 seats, becoming the largest party. The SCP collapsed, and Honda formed a PPO-led minority government.

=== 59th Diet (2021–2025) ===
The 59th Diet was led by Prime Minister Honda's minority government. Its defining achievement was the passage of the [[National Universal Health Act]] in 2023. This landmark, and highly controversial, single-payer healthcare bill became the central political issue of the term, with the LDP opposition focusing on its high cost and associated tax increases.

=== 60th Diet (2025–2026) ===
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2025|Honda-Nakayama Cabinet}}

The [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] resulted in a major political realignment. The PPO, while remaining the largest party, suffered a 20-seat loss. The LDP, under new leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], had a major resurgence, gaining 40 seats to become the clear Leader of the Opposition.

The most dramatic shift was the "{{Nihongo|Nakayama-mania|ナカヤマ・フィーバー|Nakayama Fībā}}" phenomenon, which saw the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]] surge from 2 seats to 50 under its new 32-year-old leader, [[Rento Nakayama]]. Lacking a majority, Prime Minister Honda formed a coalition government between the PPO and the OSP (231 seats), appointing Nakayama as Minister of Health and Welfare.

The Honda-Nakayama Cabinet collapsed on 12 March 2026 following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the ensuing "{{Nihongo|March Maelstrom|三月の旋風|Sangatsu no Senpū}}" of civil unrest. The LDP passed a motion of no confidence, triggering the dissolution of the House and a snap election.

=== 61st Diet (2026–present) ===
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2026|Ikazaki Cabinet}}

The [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]] was held on 17 May 2026. The election produced a decisive leftward realignment of Sanese politics:

* The PPO suffered a historic collapse, losing 118 seats and falling from 181 to 63—its worst result since the party's founding.
* The SCP under [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] achieved a dramatic surge, gaining 70 seats to reach 115.
* The OSP under Nakayama doubled its representation from 50 to 100 seats.
* The LDP under Sadanuma declined to 110 seats.
* The SDP gained 11 seats to reach 36.

The three left-wing parties—SCP (115), OSP (100), and SDP (36)—formed a coalition government with 251 seats, 13 more than the 238 required for an absolute majority. [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] became Prime Minister, with Nakayama as Deputy Prime Minister, leading the [[Ikazaki Cabinet|110th Cabinet of Okaiken]]. One independent, [[Hayashi Akihiro]] (Ogasawara-3rd), pledged confidence and supply to the coalition, giving a working majority of 252.

The Ikazaki Cabinet's day-one executive orders included the establishment of the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] (an independent rail safety body with grounding authority), the initiation of criminal prosecution against private infrastructure contractor Kizan, and the Two-Man Mandate requiring a minimum of two crew members on all rail services. The coalition's legislative agenda includes the [[Digital Sovereignty Act]], the SDP's flagship bill requiring analogue backup systems for all critical infrastructure.

== Electoral system ==
The 475 members of the House of Commoners are elected through a parallel voting system. The 475 seats are divided into two groups, and each voter casts two ballots:

* '''Single-Member Constituencies:''' 289 members are elected from single-member districts under the first-past-the-post system. Voters cast a ballot for a local candidate.
* '''Proportional Blocks:''' 186 members are elected from 11 regional proportional representation blocks. Voters cast a second ballot for a political party's list. Seats in each block are distributed proportionally to the parties based on the percentage of votes each party received in that block.

=== Right to vote and candidature ===
* Sanese nationals aged 18 years and older may vote.
* Sanese nationals aged 25 years and older may run for office in the House of Commoners.

